<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Index Librorum Prohibitorum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1560">1560</a>–<a href="/years/1966">1966</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Index of Forbidden Books, Index</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Index Librorum Prohibitorum, or Index of Forbidden Books, was a changing list of publications that the Catholic Church deemed heretical or contrary to morality, which Catholics were forbidden to print or read. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-paul-iv">Pope Paul IV</a> a Roman Index was published in 1559, banning the entire works of some 550 authors, and from 1571 the Sacred Congregation of the Index kept it up to date. It condemned religious and secular works alike, among them Kepler's Epitome astronomiae Copernicanae and Kant's Critique of Pure Reason. The last edition, of 1948, listed 4,000 titles; in 1966 the Church declared that the Index no longer had the force of ecclesiastical law.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/index-librorum-prohibitorum.jpg" alt="Index Librorum Prohibitorum" width="330" height="515" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Index_Librorum_Prohibitorum_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…1524 and remained in the Catholic Church, yet after his death his works were placed on the papal <b>Index of Prohibited Books</b> of 1559; Thomas More, who wrote against the reformers, was executed in 1535 for refusing to accept Henry VIII as head of the Church in England.</blockquote><a href="/movement/renaissance-humanism">Renaissance humanism · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>The Church’s ban on Copernican books was relaxed only gradually: the general prohibition was dropped in 1758, and Copernicus’s book and Galileo’s Dialogue left the <b>Index of Prohibited Books</b> in 1835; in 1992 Pope John Paul II acknowledged the errors of Galileo’s judges.</blockquote><a href="/movement/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution · Outcomes</a></li></ul></section>
